{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/987277-writer-s-block-cabal-ideas-for-the-founding-mages-of-philadelphia","id":987277,"title":"Writer's Block - Cabal ideas for the 'founding' Mages of Philadelphia","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Mage: The Awakening"],"posts":[{"date":"2016-10-14T08:55:33-07:00","id":987277,"title":"Writer's Block - Cabal ideas for the 'founding' Mages of Philadelphia","content":"You know. The Mages of Great Britain and Europe who braved the travel of the Atlantic and the many hardships and dangers of the New World who are honored still today for their contribution in laying the foundation of some of the first uniquely American Caucuses, thereby carrying on the legacy of the Great Orders into the present time. This will be my first time ever running Mage, and I'm using the new edition of course. I'm struggling to come up with some good Cabal names and themes for my Philadelphia game, to hopefully add some dimension to its 'secret' history. Any ideas?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7876-aiwass","name":"Aiwass","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7876_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"45"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-14T09:38:18-07:00","id":987310,"title":"","content":"I would look at the Masons for ideas.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6084-2pttakrill","name":"2ptTakrill","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"942"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-14T10:55:58-07:00","id":987334,"title":"","content":"Dan Brown could be a fine source of inspiration for Philadelphia, perhaps sprinkled with a little of National Treasure, just for fun.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/216-zooroos","name":"Zooroos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar216_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"250"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-14T14:32:26-07:00","id":987447,"title":"","content":"Three ideas:<br /> <br /> A major Silver Ladder cabal centered around the Sisterhood of the Blessed: Enlightenment-inspired women who run a Cryptopoly of local associations as high society wives. Later mages have doggedly searched for evidence of a similarly influential cabal of thearch men, but no one was as enduring or widespread as the Sisterhood until well into the 19th century.<br /> <br /> An Adamantine Arrow/Mysterium cabal with a James Fenimore Cooper sort of feel: initially a hesitant but fruitful collaboration between Lenape and European mages, by the time of the Revolutionary War it was exclusively European and claimed to steward and carry on the Awakened magical traditions of the Lenape cabals better than they themselves could. Lots of ritual costumes and traditions that a 21st century mage would find deeply, deeply uncomfortable.<br /> <br /> A Guardians of the Veil cabal that runs a Labyrinth of Mason-esque mystery cults for ambitious Philadelphians: not only do they test for the Awakening, they help the Sleeper political careers of those they deem to be wise rulers and sabotage the efforts of the unworthy. Up to you if the cabal leans monarchist or republican and how they define &quot;worthy.&quot;","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>2E Legacy Updates</b><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/978498-2e-legacy-update-brotherhood-of-the-demon-wind\" target=\"_blank\">Brotherhood of the Demon Wind</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/979249-2e-legacy-update-choir-of-hashmallim-with-bonus-content-about-summoning\" target=\"_blank\">Choir of Hashmallim</a> (plus extra Summoning content)<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/980550-2e-legacy-update-storm-keepers\" target=\"_blank\">Storm Keepers</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2207-caladriu","name":"Caladriu","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1048"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-14T14:48:25-07:00","id":987452,"title":"","content":"See <b>Boston Unveiled</b>, it has cool historical portion on setting. I imagine you could easily have off shoot of Stone Assembly Mysterium cabal of it - or Epona's Chariot for Silver Ladder. More to see in <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/mage-the-awakening/784966-2ed-boston-new-england-setting?p=874334#post874334\">our fan Boston 2E setting notes</a>. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/wink.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Wink\" smilieid=\"4\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1437-wyrdhamster","name":"wyrdhamster","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1437_5.jpg","title":"Banned","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11313"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-14T19:06:33-07:00","id":987541,"title":"","content":"wyrdhamster&gt; I'm trying to avoid ripping off Boston: Unveiled, for better or worse.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7876-aiwass","name":"Aiwass","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7876_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"45"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-14T19:28:24-07:00","id":987553,"title":"","content":"I like the idea of one named the Tea Party in an ironic fashion. I suppose it would fit more for the Free Council than anything.<br /> <br /> I'm having a hard time coming up with Cabal and Shadow names as well, plus I don't know anything about Philadelphia but oh my I just remembered Hunter's first edition books were set in Philadelphia. I'll take a quick glance through that.<br /> <br /> There's some stuff based around the Lenape, Charles Redheffer and his perpetual motion machine. The Antiquarian Society seems to be a Mysterium cabal. There's also apparently a rising of 'Hoodoo Queens,' which just sounds like a great cabal name. There was a group called the Bona Dea who apparently tried to offer the US Navy stealth transport. I like the idea of the Philadelphia Experiments for another Free Council name. There's an all-female group called the Daughters of Diana. The Shadow-Touched who might be a scelesti cabal but still have a nice name. A greedy group called the Brotherhood of the Comet who apparently convince rich people to commit suicide 'with the promise that they will be resurrected and &quot;blossom&quot; with their own magical powers.' While actually bilking them out of their cash. Another group called the Undesirables who just wanted to have fun, lost control and regretted it. They seem to be working on fixing it, though are blacklisted by the rest of the community.<br /> <br /> And here I am re-reading this and you mean the 'founding' mages. I suppose you would want to link it to geographical areas, the Lenape and the colonists.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":886,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","user":"nofather","date":". 10-14-2016, 10:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2016-10-15T15:21:30-07:00","id":987957,"title":"","content":"How about one named &quot;The Brotherhood&quot; that focuses on the sharing of information (controlled however they deem appropriate; I'm thinking Quaker more than Free Council, here. Recall why Philadelphia is so named.) and serving humanity to the best of their ability. They'd probably focus on non-violent solutions to things, which would be sorely tested at several points. It would probably break, eventually, though the ideals may spread out.<br /> <br /> Given the religious persecution and alienation that the various colonies dealt with, I would absolutely <i>expect</i> that Nameless cabals outnumbered the Diamond pretty early on, and that many of these were feeders into what became the Free Council of Assemblies. In fact, I imagine that the ultimate form that the FC take was tested by multiple cabals around the more idealistic areas... which described Philadelphia. They were experimenting with different governance throughout the colonies and around them. That had to spill back into the mage's ideas.<br /> <br /> It was a time of great change---Exactly the zeitgeist that the Free Council now seeks to recapture and nurture so it never ends. This is a modern focus because the explosion that happened then <i>did</i> end... and those who held the ideals that faded morn the end. So, by the various books, you are dealing with a very idealistic period. (Look up Shakers and Quakers and colonial mission work to get a feel for the change going on.)<br /> <br /> That said, these people didn't argue for no regulation, they argued for self government. This actually raises an individual's responsibility. The Guardians of the Veil could easily be true believers in the non-violence, but, as a <b>result</b>, be far more bloodthirsty in trying to protect their precious enlightened people. After all, these folks are so close to becoming something great, they need our help.<br /> <br /> Remember the missionaries who delivered blankets to the natives? They were <i>honestly trying to <b>help</b> </i>them. Take a mystery religion about doing the nasty, but necessary, things so others don't have to dirty their hands, and you get exactly the kind of thinking that came up with that horror. That's the relationship I could see the Guardians having with the mages of Philadelphia who are trying to become perfect sage-guardians of humanity.<br /> <br /> Also, recall that there were native mages, too. They had their own cabals. (lodges, houses, or other translations of their native terms for it, of course) The depopulation that struck means that there are probably many places where the mages are literally the only survivors, and others where the few who made it were because of magic. These things weren't linked inextricably to the importing of natives to their shores. (It was the result of an invasion thousands of miles away.) Still, some would resent and others would consider it as inevitable. Trade one set of obnoxious neighbors for another? Eh.<br /> <br /> In an area like Philadelphia, you'd have more free interaction between natives and colonists than most picture. (actually, that's generally true) There were trade settlements and natives incorporated into colonies. There were colonists adopted into tribes and left the colonies. There was a <i>lot</i> of exchange of ideas. (Study folk remedies to get a good feel for that.)<br /> <br /> That means that there were exchanges of mystical knowledge, over time. (In real life, by the by, some of that was due to the fact that whites were immune to and unable to use magic, according to multiple tribes. Some claimed this was due to a lack of a soul. There were theories, but the rule was that it worked on other tribes but not whites. If you want to incorporate that into your game as a Mystery, it could be interesting.)<br /> <br /> Native cabals would be largely familial and very exclusive to the tribe. (Which means it's normally easier to be <i>adopted into a tribe</i> than to have them bend that rule for you.)<br /> They would be focused on helping the tribe plan agriculture, hunts, war, and deal with supernatural threats. (Hence treating magic as a military secret) Thus, while very different from the incoming magi, they could still have traditions that were identifiably Atlantean or even some specific order(s). It seems likely that most tribes wouldn't have the orders represented as external groups, but instead, as internal factions. Some lodge within the tribe might do things differently than another, after all. <br /> <br /> That's my first thoughts on the matter.<br /> <br /> I might circle back with more, later. For now, off to hunt the wild gyoza... or at least the ingredients for same.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Grump, grouse, and/or gripe.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/672-thenate","name":"thenate","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar672_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"928"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}